1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
I'm Brian Bluyn and I am a cup master with PAC 3285 out of Rockford, Michigan.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This edition of the Scoutmaster podcast is sponsored by backers like me

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
And now the old Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Hey, everybody, this is Clark Green.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
It's summertime and so our next few podcasts feature on-core presentations from our archives,

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
And this time around I want to play an interview that I conducted with Mike Malone,

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
who's the author of a really fantastic book called 4%.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
It's a history of the Eagle Award

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
And, as you may imagine, Mike's an eagle scout, so he knows what he's talking about.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
It has a long tenure in scouting and produced a incredibly well researched

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
and very compelling book about eagle scouts.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
As always, this podcast is only possible because of the generosity of the folks

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
who become ScoutmasterCGcom backers.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Go to ScoutmasterCGcom, click the support link at the top of the page.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
You'll find out how you can become a ScoutmasterCGcom backer.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
We'll be back with our regular schedule of podcasts in a few weeks.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Until then, let's get started, shall we?

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Michael S Malone is one of the world's best-known technology writers.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
He was twice nominated for the Pulitzer Prize for investigative reporting

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
And you've read his articles and editorials and publications like The Wall Street Journal,

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
The Economist, Fortune and The New York Times.

22
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
He's the author and co-author of nearly 20 award-winning books.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Mike is, all importantly, an Eagle Scout and currently an assistant scoutmaster

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
of Troop 466 in Sunnyvale, California.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Now, in this centennial year of 2012,- the centennial year of the Eagle Scout-

26
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Mike turned his considerable talent as an investigative reporter and writer,

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
as well as his extensive experience as a scout and adult volunteer,

28
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
towards a book that no doubt will stand, at least in my humble opinion,

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
as the definitive history of the Eagle Scout Award.

30
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
It's called 4% and subtitled the Story of Uncommon Youth in a Century of American Life.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
I think it's not only an authoritative history of the award,

32
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
but it's kind of a meditation on the meaning and the spirit and importance of it,

33
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
And I'm certainly honored to welcome to the podcast.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Thanks for joining us, Mike.

35
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Well, thank you, Clair.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
So in the first chapter of the book, you call the Eagle the PhD of boyhood.

37
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Yeah, I heard that along the way somewhere a few years ago

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and I thought it was a brilliant metaphor for what Eagle Scouting is,

39
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:03,000
that it's a combination of learned skills combined with a final culminating event,

40
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
which is the Eagle Service Project that has to contribute to the larger world.

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
And if you look at it that way. it really is like a doctorate

42
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
and there's nothing comparable to it in American boyhood

43
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:23,000
There never has been. and it's really the apogee of being a teenage boy in the United States

44
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
And it's been that way for 100 years now.

45
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
What I really enjoy about the book- because it's a great read,

46
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:35,000
because it's a fantastic story. just the story of how this award came to be

47
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
and who has received it and what they went on to do is just a fascinating story.

48
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:48,000
And I think in one wise the history of the award itself is a history of the Boy Scouts of America.

49
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
We were talking just a little bit before we started the interview here

50
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
that that history has its ups and downs and its bumps and its troubles.

51
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
That's one of the things I really appreciate about your work.

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
It doesn't dodge any of those issues.

53
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,000
It's very honest.

54
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Did you struggle with any of that?

55
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Yeah, when I set out to write this book, I'm a lifelong newspaper man

56
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Journalist. I'm a fourth generation newspaper man.

57
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
In fact I'm sitting here editing some copy from my 21-year-old son

58
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
who is now a fifth generation family newspaper man.

59
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,000
So it's in the blood

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
And it was very hard for me to even contemplate the idea of whitewashing this story

61
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
or turning into a hagiography of the March of Great Men.

62
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
That's just not my style.

63
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
I think the story is much more interesting when you capture awards and all,

64
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
And it's not like Scouting has a lot of awards.

65
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
I mean, it's an inspiring story all the way through

66
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
And, yeah, there have been some missteps and trips down the wrong path a few times,

67
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
But all in all I think those other things, those darker things,

68
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
actually make the entire story much more spectacular, much more amazing.

69
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
It's an awe-inspiring story?

70
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Yeah, it really is,

71
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
And those bright moments are offset by the not-so-bright ones.

72
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Let me tell you the genesis of this book, because it's kind of unusual.

73
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
I had taken a group of scouts from my troop back to Northern Virginia.

74
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Two of the boys were restoring a Civil War battlefield and cemetery,

75
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
which sounds hugely ambitious

76
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
And it was, even though these are the two smallest battlefields

77
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
and federal cemeteries in the United States- Balls Bluff.

78
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
But it was quite an experience doing this

79
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
And as part of it we were going to be in the flag ceremony

80
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
in our Civil War uniforms at the National Jamboree

81
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
And we also marched in the Centennial Parade of Boy Scouting

82
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
down Pennsylvania Avenue in Washington DC

83
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
And I think there was 20,000 scouts in that parade.

84
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
It was quite a parade.

85
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
It was the first one of this kind since the first jamboree before FDR.

86
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
I was in the contingent at the very end, which was Eagle Scouts,

87
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
And each of us was asked to carry or wear something

88
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
that represented our career.

89
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
So I had the notebook, you know. I had the satchel over my shoulder

90
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
with the press card. but I looked around me

91
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
and there were guys dressed as department stores, Santa Claus.

92
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
There was a guy from Bermuda who was a diplomat.

93
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
There was a military officer and he was wearing

94
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
the full Hurt Locker bomb demolition outfit-

95
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
which was pretty tough because it was 100 degrees that day-

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Just a panoply of distinguished middle-aged and older men

97
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
showing the wares of these remarkable careers

98
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
that had followed becoming Eagle Scouts.

99
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
And we started down that parade.

100
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
I was afraid a few of us weren't going to make it in that heat.

101
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
There were 80-year-olds with us marching at the tail end

102
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
of this massive parade of Boy Scouts.

103
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
We turned the corner and started heading down

104
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
towards the Washington Monument

105
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
And as we did, the loudspeaker shouted out

106
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,000
ladies and gentlemen, America's Eagle Scouts.

107
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And the roar that came up from the crowd.

108
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
There was probably 50 or 60,000 people

109
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
lining that mile of avenue there,

110
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
The roar that came up for these Eagles,

111
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
And looking at these old guys with bad hips

112
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
and blimping and all that, and we all stood

113
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
a whole lot straighter and marched in formation

114
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
And I suddenly was struck by the relationship

115
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
between Eagle Scouts and American culture

116
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
And that story had never really been explored

117
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
And I just had to write the book at that point

118
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
It was an insane idea because I was buried

119
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
in a new startup technology company

120
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
and writing other books and teaching college.

121
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
But I just had to write the book

122
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
because I realized nobody else was going to do it.

123
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
There were no real plans for the 100th anniversary

124
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,000
of Eagle Scouting, so I decided to do the celebration myself

125
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
And the result shows the passion that you had for it.

126
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Yeah, it's kind of a book that wrote itself.

127
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
I tore through this one.

128
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Some books are very painful to write.

129
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Spending three years writing about Steve Jobs once

130
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
was not the happiest time of my life,

131
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,000
But writing this book my fingers worked by themselves.

132
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
If we asked everybody who the first Eagle Scout was,

133
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
they'd probably come up with Arthur Eldred.

134
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
But there's a lot more to that story

135
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
and there's a lot more nuance and depth to it.

136
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:48,000
First tell me about the time that Arthur was a scout

137
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and what was going on at that point in the history of the BSA.

138
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Yeah, let me preface that by saying

139
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:00,000
we all know it's Arthur Eldred. but 15,, 20 years ago

140
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
I think, if you asked people who the first Eagle Scout was,

141
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
nobody would know.

142
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
I don't think the BSA was quite certain

143
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,000
And that was a fact from basically the 20s until the 90s

144
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
that people didn't really know about Arthur Eldred

145
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
until I think it was Boy's Life

146
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
or Scouting Magazine went out to find who it was

147
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And even then they got the wrong photo.

148
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
For a number of years there was this square-jawed kid

149
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,000
out of a poster who everyone thought was Arthur Eldred.

150
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
And finally they found real photos of the guy

151
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
and he looks just like every other Eagle Scout

152
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
you've ever known in your life,

153
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Kind of skinny, smart-looking as eyes

154
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,000
and a bit amused having to pose for the photograph.

155
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
You can really empathize.

156
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
I mean, Arthur Eldred is us,

157
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
except he's kind of more than the rest of us

158
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
given his career.

159
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
He got his Eagle in 1912,

160
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
and you have to understand that Scouting

161
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
didn't begin as a monolithic organization.

162
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,000
It flows out of multiple organizations,

163
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
the Daniel Boone Society, various other groups.

164
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:13,000
It starts out as basically a fad among teenage boys.

165
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
They hear about Boy Scouts in England

166
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
and they start their own groups and they turn into troops

167
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,000
and they turn into these sort of ad hoc organizations.

168
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
There were about four or five different Boy Scouts organizations

169
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
in the first decade of the 20th century.

170
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
They were all over the place.

171
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
And if you ever want to read the whole history of it

172
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
or read the Scouting Party,

173
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
which is the story of these early days

174
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
and the clashes of egos of the different founders

175
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,000
and all of that.

176
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
So Arthur Eldred was a boy living on Long Island.

177
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
His father had died.

178
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
His mother was concerned, like mothers today,

179
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
that without a dad he would get in trouble.

180
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
He had an older brother who was quite a bit older

181
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
who sort of stepped in as his surrogate dad

182
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
and helped rape him.

183
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
And this older brother looked around

184
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
and saw these emerging scouting groups

185
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
and thought, well, why don't I build one of these groups

186
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
and with Arthur in it,

187
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and that'll give structure to his life?

188
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
So he went out and actually set out

189
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
to join another Scouting organization,

190
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
one that's long gone now,

191
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
but it was much more of a militaristic organization.

192
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
They carried guns around, they marched

193
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and they were sort of like preparing boys to join the army.

194
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,000
It's not a bad thing to do in 1910

195
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
given the clouds forming over Europe.

196
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
But he had trouble getting permission to start

197
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
the organization or something

198
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
and instead he said, okay, well,

199
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,000
there's this other outfit at the headquarter down in Manhattan,

200
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Why don't we join up with them?

201
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
and that was the Boy Scouts of America.

202
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
So they started this troop out on Long Island

203
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
and the boys did the usual stuff.

204
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
They didn't have a lot of money.

205
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
They sort of pooled their dough to buy uniforms.

206
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Some of them didn't have uniforms.

207
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
It was very, very casual.

208
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
It was sort of closer to Baden Powell's dream

209
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:12,000
of what scouting was

210
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
than what we think of today

211
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
of 60-member scout troops

212
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
meeting in a church gymnasium

213
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
with a trailer full of stuff, going camping

214
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and all that.

215
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
And this was boys meeting in the evening

216
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
like in a barn or something.

217
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And Arthur, at the time

218
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
there was very few documentation

219
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
on how to be a Boy Scout.

220
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
There weren't really any ranks at the time.

221
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
It was all in development.

222
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:46,000
And so when the first Boy Scout handbook appeared,

223
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
boys across America-

224
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
there was probably 100,000 boys across the United States

225
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
who were involved in the organization at that time.

226
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
they gobbled up that book

227
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
because it not only showed them all the scouting skills,

228
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
I mean, they had been dealing, working with Baden Powell's old book

229
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
and various other books about the outdoors.

230
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Now they had an official book

231
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
and they went at it,

232
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:12,000
They consumed it and began earning the various ranks.

233
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
And the ranks were kind of what they are today,

234
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
up until first class.

235
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
So you had 10% class first class

236
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
And then it all kind of went crazy

237
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
because there were merit badges,

238
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
but there was only about 15 merit badges

239
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and they were divided in two different categories.

240
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
There was sort of like an outdoor category

241
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
and then there was the home and citizenship category

242
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
And you either earned the star or the life.

243
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:38,000
They weren't sequential,

244
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
They were kind of in opposition.

245
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
And then there was this thing called the Eagle Scout Award

246
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
And this was the first time these boys had ever seen it

247
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:47,000
And they didn't.

248
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
Boy Scouts didn't give a lot of information,

249
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
They just sort of said you had to have a certain number of merit badges.

250
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:58,000
and they had a drawing of the Eagle Medal

251
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:02,000
And it was very different from what we think of as the Eagle Medal.

252
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
It was a straight ribbon with one color, monochromatic.

253
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,000
They didn't know what color it was.

254
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,000
It was a black and white book

255
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
And an eagle in flight flying sideways

256
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
And that's all they knew.

257
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
And there weren't really enough merit badges

258
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
to get to the Eagle yet

259
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
Available, but Boy Scouts promised they'd have more.

260
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
So that's kind of where things stood.

261
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
So all across America boys were earning these ranks.

262
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Well, Arthur Elder was one of these singular characters

263
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:31,000
And he sort of is the embodiment of what we think of

264
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
as that overachieving Eagle Scout.

265
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Eagle Scouts are inevitably-

266
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
I've mentored about 50 of them.

267
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
I know you have mentored probably at least that many.

268
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,000
These kids are focused and they go for it.

269
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
And if scouting is said you have to have 150 merit badges,

270
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
we still have Eagle Scouts

271
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
because these are the kinds of young men

272
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:56,000
who are goal-oriented and they'll do it. whatever it takes,

273
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
However many hours and how much blood, sweat and tears,

274
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
they'll get there.

275
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
Well, Arthur saw the number, the 21 merit badges,

276
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
which was even true then-

277
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and went out and got as many merit badges he could possibly get.

278
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
And then, when scouting finally announced

279
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
the next set of merit badges,

280
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
he went out and got those.

281
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
And he got them at a pace that is unprecedented

282
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and will never again be equal

283
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
At one point, as near as I can figure,

284
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,000
when he had about 17 or 18 merit badges.

285
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
he had more merit badges, I believe,

286
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:36,000
than all the other Scouts in the United States.

287
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
So it's sort of like Babe Ruth.

288
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
You know, Babe Ruth in the early 1920s

289
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
one year had more home runs

290
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
than the entire rest of the American League.

291
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Well, that's kind of what Arthur Elder was like 15 years before that.

292
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,000
He just simply fell in love with getting merit badges

293
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
and he got them all.

294
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Well, now he had a problem

295
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
because no one knew what he was supposed to do next,

296
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
including the voiceouts from America.

297
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
So it said, you know, they said:

298
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
well, you have to have approval for all of this

299
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
from your local leaders.

300
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
So he ran around and got

301
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
letters of recommendation from all the local civic figures

302
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
in his town in Long Island

303
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
and he sent those in.

304
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Well, at this point Scouting was rocked on its heels

305
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
because it had not expected this to happen.

306
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:30,000
Scouting has a tendency to announce these major new initiatives

307
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:34,000
and then is surprised when these over-achieving voiceouts

308
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:38,000
earn these things long before the institution's prepared for them.

309
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:43,000
He finishes the Eagle, he notifies Scout headquarters in Manhattan

310
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
and all hell breaks with him

311
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:50,000
and he goes. oh no, we weren't even expecting an Eagle for five more years

312
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
and this kid comes along and he's got it.

313
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
What are we going to do now?

314
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:00,000
They invite him down to New York City

315
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
and they give him a border review.

316
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And obviously this is the institution saying

317
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
we better make sure this kid is actually for real

318
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
because we're going to have to announce him

319
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
as the first Eagle Scout in history

320
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
and we hope he's presentable.

321
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
So they bring him down

322
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and they put him into a border review

323
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
with the founders of Scouting- all of them.

324
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,000
We wear medals named after him,

325
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:33,000
like the Voice Award, and all the rest of these guys,

326
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:39,000
these legends, and somehow he went into this border review

327
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
and came out alive.

328
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
It must have been very much like a Star Chamber.

329
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
I don't think any Eagle Scout- I'm sure

330
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
no other Eagle Scout in history-

331
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
had to face a border review like this one.

332
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
Well, is this? am I right?

333
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
Do I remember reading that Ernest Thompson Seaton

334
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
is on this board and Dan Beard?

335
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Yeah, I remember Ernest Thompson Seaton and Dan Beard

336
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
hated each other

337
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,000
because they had invented the two founding organizations

338
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
that became Boy Scouts that, in fact,

339
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
the Brits had copied.

340
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
The original book on the outdoors

341
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
was the one that Baden Powell lifted from

342
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
to create the original Boy Scout handbook.

343
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
So these middle-aged men sitting there

344
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
facing Arthur Eldred had all sorts of

345
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
access to grind and feuds against each other

346
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
and everything else.

347
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:34,000
To my mind, as impressive as Arthur Eldred's achievement-

348
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
earning all those merit patches- was

349
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:40,000
surviving that group may have been his.

350
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
that may have qualified him more than anything else.

351
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:46,000
He must have been an amazing negotiator.

352
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:50,000
So he walks out, the very first Eagle Scout.

353
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
Well, now Scouting's got another problem.

354
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Nobody ever made the medal.

355
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
They've got a drawing.

356
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
That's literally all they have.

357
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
All they have is the drawing and the Boy Scout handbook.

358
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
And so

359
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,000
And so Boyz has to send a note to Arthur Eldred saying

360
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
the medal isn't ready yet.

361
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:13,000
Can you wait until fall?

362
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
So what's Eldred going to do?

363
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
Fine, Yes, I'll wait until fall.

364
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
Well, while he's waiting,

365
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:25,000
he goes off to Boy Scout camp with his troop

366
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
And in the middle of his camp,

367
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
swimming in a lake,

368
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
one of the boys starts to drown.

369
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
Eldred goes in after him,

370
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
along with another scout,

371
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
to save the kid.

372
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
Well, as often happens,

373
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,000
as they teach you in life-saving, as you remember,

374
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
people that go in to save people

375
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
sometimes get into the stress themselves.

376
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:50,000
So Arthur goes in, he saves the kid that's drowning.

377
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
but now he's got the other kid who's starting to drown

378
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
and so he saves him too.

379
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:59,000
So we have this amazing character

380
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
who earns more merit badges

381
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,000
than anybody in all of Boy Scouts combined.

382
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
He then survives this star chamber

383
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
with the founders of scouting for his border review.

384
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
Then, while he's waiting to get his award,

385
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:17,000
he earns the honor medal for life-saving twice.

386
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:21,000
And this is all in the course of, you know, a summer.

387
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
So Arthur had a pretty good year.

388
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:28,000
Come fall they finally give him the Eagle Medal

389
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and it's completely different

390
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
than the one in the book. thank goodness,

391
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
because we end up with.

392
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
you can see the medal even now

393
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:40,000
it's in the museum, in the Boy Scout Museum in Texas.

394
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
They give him the medal that looks

395
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
almost exactly like the one we all wear now.

396
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:49,000
So that's Arthur Eldred, a remarkable man,

397
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:53,000
And goes on and stays involved

398
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
all the way up through the 30s

399
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
and sits on boards of reviews.

400
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
Yeah, he becomes a very

401
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,000
you think. well, he's going to be like Neil Armstrong.

402
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
you know he's going to do a few things,

403
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
but in a sense, once again

404
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,000
he embodies what being an Eagle Scout is.

405
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:13,000
He becomes this responsible, trusted citizen

406
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:16,000
who sits on various civic committees.

407
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:20,000
His job is on these various industrial boards,

408
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
you know, for quality and that sort of thing,

409
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:27,000
just live a solid, honest life of a good citizen

410
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
and gets back involved in scouting

411
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,000
and his son becomes an Eagle Scout

412
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:36,000
And there's a picture of Arthur in his 50s,

413
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
you know, with gray hair

414
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:41,000
and giving the award to his son

415
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
And the line of Eldred's ever since

416
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
has earned Eagle Scouts.

417
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:48,000
I think we're fourth or fifth-generation Eldred's now

418
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
with Eagle Metal.

419
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:54,000
But after he earns the medal in 1912,

420
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:57,000
he just sort of fades from scouting history

421
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:00,000
until 50, 60 years later

422
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
they begin to. you know,

423
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:03,000
like a lot of institutions,

424
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
they don't start thinking about their history

425
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
until they have some major milestone

426
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
And scouting and it's 50th anniversary,

427
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
really begin to think about its past

428
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
and start to try to document it

429
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
and discovered it had lost a lot of its records

430
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:19,000
And it took a while to find it was Arthur

431
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:20,000
and then it took even longer

432
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
to actually find a photo of him.

433
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Every honor he gets he deserves.

434
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:24,000
He's an amazing person.

435
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
Well, he kind of invented the Eagle,

436
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
you know. in some ways,

437
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:29,000
I mean, and what he did

438
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,000
he was just as much an inventor of it

439
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
as anybody else, it seems.

440
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:35,000
Oh, absolutely

441
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:36,000
Yeah.

442
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:37,000
Oftentimes these individuals.

443
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:41,000
they force institutions to respond to them

444
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
and in doing so

445
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:48,000
they drive the definition of what something is.

