473
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
Arthur's story of going on

474
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
kind of leads into the idea that

475
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
by the 1920s

476
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
these boys who were earning Eagle Scout,

477
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:51,000
they would become 18,

478
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,000
they would age out of the organization.

479
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
But there was some response to that

480
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:01,000
and it resulted in an organization

481
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
that used to be very well known,

482
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:04,000
but I don't know that anybody

483
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,000
really knows about it anymore-

484
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
the Knights of Dunamis.

485
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:08,000
How did that all come about?

486
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
And you're talking to one

487
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
of the last surviving Knights-

488
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:13,000
Really

489
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:16,000
There aren't many of us left.

490
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
I got my Eagle very young

491
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
and I became a Knight of Dunamis,

492
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:24,000
I think when I was 16 or 17,.

493
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
stayed until I was 21,,

494
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,000
and that was the early 1970s,

495
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:32,000
And it was basically an organization

496
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:33,000
to take Eagle Scouts

497
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
and to organize them and use them

498
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
to perform service to Scouting

499
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:38,000
and to the community,

500
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
Not really from the time you became an Eagle,

501
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
but usually from the time you left your troop

502
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
and you joined a chapter

503
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
and ultimately there was about 50 chapters

504
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
or 100 chapters around the United States,

505
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
several thousand members.

506
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
I was a chapter president in San Jose

507
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:55,000
in the late 60s, early 70s

508
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
and we did everything from

509
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
honor guard in the Cinco de Mayo

510
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
parade to putting on children's

511
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Christmas shows in hospitals,

512
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:08,000
to holding probably 100

513
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:09,000
Eagle Court of Honors.

514
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:10,000
we would preside over them

515
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
with our robes on and all that.

516
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
So it was really a remarkable organization

517
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:17,000
that did a tremendous amount of things.

518
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
Amazing organization.

519
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
It's what really made me think of myself

520
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
as an Eagle Scout.

521
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
But by the 1970s and 80s

522
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
it was fading.

523
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
Scouting gave it one last chance

524
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
and by then it was too late.

525
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,000
And so I found myself

526
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,000
at the 1973 National Jamboree

527
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
In Pennsylvania standing in front of about

528
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
10,000 Eagle Scouts

529
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
announcing the death. I was a part of the program.

530
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
I announced the death of the Knights of Dunamis

531
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:48,000
and the founding of the National Eagle Scout Association.

532
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,000
And you're still involved in that.

533
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
Yeah, I'm the president of the local NISA chapter,

534
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:57,000
Much more structured than the Knights of Dunamis was.

535
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
I missed the old Knights of Dunamis

536
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
and my hope is that NISA,

537
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
as it develops, will become more

538
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
like that, more service-oriented,

539
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
become an institution itself.

540
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
And I think the first really good step

541
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
was the creation of the Adams Award,

542
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
which is that award for the best

543
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
Eagle project in the United States.

544
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
That's a pretty recent thing, yeah,

545
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
Yeah, it's just three years old

546
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
And, remarkably enough,

547
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
my oldest son

548
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
earned the first

549
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
Adams Award.

550
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,000
He won the award for the Western Region.

551
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,000
He actually got a bunch of Silicon Valley

552
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
CEOs and VCs to buy

553
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
a 50-foot shipping container.

554
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
He filled it full of about three schools worth of supplies

555
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,000
and sent it off to

556
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:41,000
Children's Town, AIDS Orphanage

557
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,000
and School in Zambia

558
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
where we had visited,

559
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,000
And the container itself

560
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
was turned into a library.

561
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
This is a glimpse of what

562
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:53,000
21st Century Eagle projects look like.

563
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
There's a good section of the book

564
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
about these awards and things

565
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
which are really new and not a whole lot of people

566
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
really know about.

567
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,000
But what was the genesis of the Eagle project?

568
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,000
You didn't always do a project

569
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:07,000
to become an Eagle Scout.

570
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,000
No, and in fact you know

571
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
you always ran into these old Eagle Scouts.

572
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
I was much tougher back in the old days.

573
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:15,000
Nobody had as hard as we did getting our Eagle.

574
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,000
There's a little bit of truth to that.

575
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:19,000
Earning an Eagle in the 1920s

576
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
or 30s or 40s

577
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:23,000
was actually not that difficult,

578
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:25,000
except for one merit batch-

579
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:27,000
Bird study. Nobody got that merit batch.

580
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
There are thousands

581
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,000
of elderly men

582
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
who will tell you that they never got the Eagle

583
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:35,000
because they couldn't pass bird study.

584
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:37,000
But in terms of the overall requirements,

585
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:39,000
modern scouting is much more difficult

586
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:41,000
and that has to do with the Eagle project.

587
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:43,000
There was a feeling that because scouting

588
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:45,000
was growing so fast during the baby boomer era

589
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:47,000
that a lot of kids were getting their Eagles

590
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:49,000
but not really getting deep into scouting,

591
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,000
so there was a requirement for

592
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,000
participation

593
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
in community service,

594
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
and they still exist.

595
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
Then, in the early 60s, it was decided that

596
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
no, that wasn't enough.

597
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:03,000
What we wanted the Eagles to do

598
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
was to take all the skills that they had learned

599
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
and to apply them

600
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
in some organized way

601
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,000
that required them to have a plan,

602
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:13,000
a project

603
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:15,000
and exhibit the skills of being a leader,

604
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,000
especially dealing with adults.

605
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,000
And that was the beginning

606
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:21,000
of the Eagle service project.

607
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,000
We got their Eagle in the late 60s

608
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:25,000
and they said-

609
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,000
I don't really remember,

610
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
I think we painted some benches at the park

611
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:31,000
and that was a very successful Eagle project

612
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
back in the late 60s.

613
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,000
But we have 300,000

614
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:37,000
teenage boys each year

615
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,000
performing

616
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
or creating

617
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:43,000
real, honest-to-goodness social enterprises,

618
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
doing social entrepreneurship

619
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,000
and changing the face of this country.

620
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:49,000
And because these tend to be

621
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,000
discreet, comparatively small

622
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
events

623
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,000
that get coverage in the local paper

624
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,000
but no one puts it all together

625
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
and says good lord,

626
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
these Eagle scouts

627
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,000
are the single most

628
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
important force of-

629
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:07,000
I don't know what you call it-

630
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:09,000
social improvement in the United States

631
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:11,000
at any given time.

632
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:13,000
You quoted in the book

633
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:15,000
the figure 10.5 million hours.

634
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,000
Is that right?

635
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,000
Yeah, 10.5 million hours a year

636
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
on Eagle projects.

637
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,000
And if you add it up over the history

638
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:25,000
of Eagle scouting,

639
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,000
we're looking at about 100 to 150 million hours

640
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
of

641
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
civic contribution

642
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:33,000
by Eagle projects,

643
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
which makes it the largest service project

644
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
by youth in history.

645
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:39,000
There's simply nothing like it.

646
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
Just amazing what these kids are doing nowadays.

647
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:43,000
Yeah, from the small to the big,

648
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:45,000
they all add up into this incredible force.

649
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,000
Yes, they absolutely do.

650
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
Yeah, it's just amazing.

651
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
Now we recently

652
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,000
marked the passing of Neil Armstrong,

653
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:55,000
who's got to be probably

654
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:57,000
arguably the most famous

655
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:59,000
Eagle scouts.

656
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,000
Yeah, it's Neil Armstrong,

657
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,000
And I'm in a weird position

658
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,000
of I think I'm the last

659
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:07,000
person to have worked with Neil Armstrong,

660
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:09,000
Really.

661
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
Yeah, because

662
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
when we had the book in draft,

663
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,000
we had access to

664
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
Dr Armstrong

665
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
And I asked him. I said:

666
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
I've written this chapter about you,

667
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,000
but I'm not sure it's accurate.

668
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
Would you check it out?

669
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
He was famously prickly about not dealing

670
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:29,000
with the media,

671
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:31,000
not doing any publicity.

672
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:33,000
At one point I made the mistake

673
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
of asking if he'd give me a jacket quote

674
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
and I got it immediate.

675
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,000
No, I don't do that,

676
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
But

677
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:43,000
a few weeks. you know we're coming up on

678
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,000
deadline. it's going to be close.

679
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:47,000
24 hours later he sent me back

680
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,000
three pages of email

681
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:51,000
correcting every little detail.

682
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,000
So I'm sitting in my house

683
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
emailing back and forth with Neil Armstrong-

684
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,000
which was pretty mind-boggling-

685
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,000
And then

686
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:01,000
I realized a week later,

687
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:03,000
when it was announced that he'd gone in for heart surgery,

688
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
that he had literally finished editing

689
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:07,000
his chapter for me like 48 hours

690
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,000
before he went to the hospital.

691
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,000
I assumed he was coming out.

692
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,000
Oh my gosh,

693
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,000
Or I can't believe that he would have devoted the time to this

694
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:17,000
If he thought it was that serious.

695
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:19,000
So

696
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
I put a little note on the bottom of that

697
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:23,000
chapter.

698
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,000
It's quite an amazing thing

699
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:27,000
And once again,

700
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:29,000
sort of an eagle scout

701
00:31:29,000 --> 00:31:31,000
being helpful.

702
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:33,000
The book has just dozens of different stories

703
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:35,000
about

704
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:37,000
eagles who went on to distinguish themselves

705
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:39,000
in various ways,

706
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:41,000
And some of them used to be pretty much

707
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:43,000
household names

708
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:45,000
And you bring them back to memory,

709
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:47,000
and one that I wanted to ask you to talk about a little bit

710
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,000
is Paul Cyple.

711
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:51,000
He's a very interesting guy

712
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:53,000
And you could argue until Neil Armstrong

713
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:55,000
comes on the scene.

714
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:57,000
The shiny example of what an eagle scout is

715
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:59,000
was Paul Cyple for

716
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:01,000
40 years,

717
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:03,000
The middle era of scouting.

718
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:05,000
He's the man,

719
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:07,000
He's the guy.

720
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:09,000
You read this and you look at your own

721
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:11,000
When you put on your eagle medal, you think,

722
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:13,000
wow, am I worthy to wear the medal

723
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:15,000
to that, Neil Armstrong

724
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:17,000
and Arthur Eldridge and Paul Cyple.

725
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:19,000
Cyple is

726
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:21,000
sort of the ultimate eagle scout.

727
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:23,000
When you think of an eagle scout he's a great big guy:

728
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,000
big, toothy grin,

729
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:27,000
affable and friendly with everyone.

730
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:29,000
A natural leader

731
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:31,000
earned his eagle, I think when he was

732
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:33,000
18 or 18 and a half.

733
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:35,000
You were on your way to eagle.

734
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,000
You were allowed to go on and finish it

735
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:39,000
after your 18th birthday.

736
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:41,000
It was already accepted into college.

737
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:43,000
I think he had 30 merit badges.

738
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:45,000
He had done a lot of 50 milers.

739
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:47,000
He was the real deal.

740
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:49,000
Commodore Perry is going to

741
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,000
go to the South Pole.

742
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,000
He publicly announced that he was going to take

743
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:55,000
one eagle scout

744
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:57,000
with him

745
00:32:57,000 --> 00:32:59,000
to Antarctica.

746
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,000
He was going to have a national competition.

747
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
Cyple hears about it and applies.

748
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:07,000
The list is swiddled down to

749
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:09,000
10 or 12 boys,

750
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:11,000
All of them

751
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:13,000
super overachievers from around the country.

752
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,000
The story is covered in every paper in America.

753
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:17,000
It's a very big deal.

754
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:19,000
Cyple shines above all of these

755
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
other overachievers and Perry agrees to take him.

756
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,000
The plan is

757
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
he's going to go along and he's going to

758
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
go on the ship, sailing down there

759
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,000
and working the galley.

760
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:31,000
By the time they get to Antarctica

761
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:33,000
he's already moved out of the galley,

762
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,000
He's up on deck and he's starting

763
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:37,000
to take care of the animals.

764
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:39,000
He turns out to be

765
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:41,000
incredibly strong

766
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:43,000
and more capable than

767
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:45,000
the professionals that Perry's brought with him.

768
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:47,000
As a result, he ends up

769
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:49,000
being Perry's number two guy

770
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,000
within about two months.

771
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:53,000
He's just this amazing guy

772
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:55,000
and everybody loves him

773
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,000
because he's such a nice guy

774
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,000
Over. he's Perry's protege

775
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,000
and when he comes back he goes to college

776
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:03,000
and he's celebrated

777
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:05,000
and fetid and all that.

778
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
Perry goes down again and is going to fly

779
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,000
over to South Pole.

780
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,000
He invites Cyple to be his guy,

781
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:13,000
to go with him down there to do it,

782
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:15,000
And so Cyple slowly becomes.

783
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
especially as Perry gets older,

784
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:19,000
Cyple becomes the world's

785
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:21,000
leading art explorer

786
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:23,000
And he not only begins

787
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:25,000
to explore Antarctica

788
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:27,000
but also the Arctic.

789
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:29,000
And when World War II

790
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,000
breaks out, he's doing

791
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:33,000
scientific research and they ask him

792
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:35,000
to investigate

793
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:37,000
different kinds of weather

794
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:39,000
systems and environments

795
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,000
for soldiers to fight in,

796
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,000
What kind of uniforms they need, and all that.

797
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:45,000
And while he's at it, he invents

798
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:47,000
the notion of windshield factor.

799
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:49,000
So whenever you hear

800
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
windshield factor, he's in front of

801
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
an NFL football game or something

802
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:55,000
and he just becomes

